w at this point here they  are going to branch off are useful  energy is  that way and a wasted energies gonna  droop down so for my useful energy my  light that's the thing I actually want  out of my device is 20 joules and each  centimeter is 10 doors the thickness of  this line is going to be 2 centimeters  so this is the bit that's important the  thickness of that that is 2 centimeters  which represents 20 joules I'm then  going to draw that going forwards a bit  again the length does not matter I'm not  even bothering to measure it my ruler  the length does not matter here it's  only the width of matters so that is my  useful energy that is my 20 jaws of  light now the 80 jaws I'm not I don't  want that thermal energy or not that's  not something that I'm desired from this  light bulb so I'm going to draw this  drooping down now because it's 80 joules  it should be 8 centimeters wide so there  we have it now this is how we do it so  from this point we should do the arrow  that's going to come down like this and  split like that so can we see they they  branched off at the same point but apart  from that it doesn't really matter the  useful one usually or nearly always goes  that way and the wasted one droops down  like this so wasted one is eighty jaws  thermal thermal store so we can see it's  all about the thickness of the arrows at  the point at which they branch off from  each other so here I have my hundred  jaws that's my input then 20 jaws is  converted to light which is my useful  energy that's why it's going that way  80 jaws can lost them that were wasted  to the thermal store so it goes down  like this and that's basically how to do  a Sankey diagram thank you
 

